<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Men (deity)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mên</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Men was a moon god worshipped widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> and Pontus, in the Hellenistic and Roman periods. He was shown as a young man in a Phrygian cap with a crescent moon behind his shoulders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> describes a sanctuary of Men Pharnakou at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amelia-umbria">Ameria</a> near Cabira, by which the kings of Pontus swore their royal oath.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/men-deity.jpg" alt="Men (deity)" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_M%C3%AAn,_the_Moon_God,_a_god_worshipped_in_the_western_interior_parts_of_Anatolia,_he_is_shown_with_a_crescent_like_open_horns_on_his_shoulders,_Roman_period,_Museum_of_Anatolian_Civilizations,_Ankara_(26438852956).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
